    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>A commercial storm claim usually has three parts: the building, business personal property (equipment and inventory), and business income or extra expense.</li>
<li>Photograph and video the damage before cleanup, then protect the property from further damage and keep every receipt.</li>
<li>Business income claims depend on financial records from before and after the loss, so gather them early.</li>
<li>Many coastal businesses have separate wind (often TWIA), flood, and commercial policies. Document wind and water damage separately.</li>
<li>Hotels, marinas, restaurants, and industrial sites each have documentation needs that general checklists miss.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>When a hurricane, hailstorm, or severe thunderstorm hits a business, the clock starts on two problems at once: repairing the property and keeping revenue coming in. Knowing how to <strong>document commercial property damage after a storm</strong> helps with both. Good documentation supports the building and contents claim, and it is the backbone of any business income claim that follows.</p>

<p>This guide is for business owners and property managers in Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and the Coastal Bend. It covers what to record, which financial documents to gather, and specific issues for hospitality, marinas, and industrial properties. It is general information. Coverage depends on your policies and the facts of your loss.</p>

<h2>The three parts of a commercial storm claim</h2>

<p>Most commercial property policies separate coverage into categories, and each category needs its own evidence.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Claim component</th><th>What it usually includes</th><th>Key documentation</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Building</td><td>Roof, walls, windows, doors, interior finishes, built-in systems, signs, fences, and other structures as listed in the policy</td><td>Photos and video, roof and drone images, moisture readings, contractor and engineer reports, repair estimates</td></tr>
<tr><td>Business personal property</td><td>Furniture, equipment, inventory, supplies, and tenant improvements where covered</td><td>Inventory lists with quantities and costs, purchase records, serial numbers, photos of damaged items</td></tr>
<tr><td>Business income</td><td>Lost net income and continuing normal operating expenses during the period of restoration, subject to the policy's terms</td><td>Profit and loss statements, tax returns, sales reports, payroll, a dated log of the interruption</td></tr>
<tr><td>Extra expense</td><td>Reasonable extra costs to avoid or reduce a shutdown, such as temporary space or equipment rental</td><td>Invoices, receipts, contracts, and a note explaining why each cost was needed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other coverages (if included)</td><td>Civil authority, utility service interruption, spoilage, debris removal, ordinance or law</td><td>Official orders, utility outage records, temperature logs, disposal receipts, permit requirements</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Read your declarations page to see which of these coverages you actually have, the limits, and any deductibles or waiting periods. Our <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> page explains how these pieces typically come together.</p>

<h2>Step 1: Document the building damage</h2>

<p>Start as soon as it is safe. Walk the exterior first, then the interior, and record everything before cleanup or temporary repairs change the scene.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Wide shots, then close-ups.</strong> Photograph each elevation of the building, then each area of damage, with something in the frame for scale.</li>
<li><strong>Roof.</strong> Low-slope commercial roofs are common in Corpus Christi. Document membrane tears, lifted flashing, displaced coping, damaged rooftop units, and ponding. Drone images can help, but never put anyone on an unsafe roof.</li>
<li><strong>Openings.</strong> Record broken glass, damaged storefronts, overhead doors, and any place where wind-driven rain entered.</li>
<li><strong>Interior water.</strong> Note ceiling stains, wet insulation, damaged finishes, and moisture readings if you have them. Mark the height of any flood water on walls before it dries.</li>
<li><strong>Signs, canopies, fences, and lighting.</strong> These are easy to forget and may be scheduled separately in the policy.</li>
</ul>

<p>Then protect the property. Reasonable temporary repairs, such as tarping, boarding windows, and water extraction, help prevent further damage. Photograph the work and keep invoices. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> is a useful companion for this step.</p>

<h3>Separate wind damage from water damage</h3>

<p>TWIA covers wind and hail only, and flood is generally excluded from commercial property policies. If a hurricane brings both wind and storm surge, the line between them becomes the most important line in your claim. Photograph roof and window openings that let rain in, and separately document the water line from rising water. Note the timing if you know it. Our article on <a href="/wind-vs-storm-surge-damage-claims-texas-coast/">wind vs. storm surge damage claims</a> explains why that matters and how the policies interact.</p>

<h2>Step 2: Document equipment and inventory</h2>

<p>Business personal property claims are often underdocumented because the owner is focused on reopening. A few habits make a big difference:</p>
<ol>
<li>Build an inventory list with item, quantity, condition, age, and cost. Start with the most valuable items.</li>
<li>Photograph damaged items in place before moving them. Capture serial numbers and model plates.</li>
<li>Pull purchase records, invoices, and point-of-sale inventory reports to support quantities and costs.</li>
<li>Do not discard damaged inventory until it is documented and the disposal is discussed with the adjuster in writing.</li>
<li>For refrigerated goods, keep temperature logs and power outage records if you have them.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Step 3: Build the business income record</h2>

<p>Business income claims are about numbers, and the insurer will want to see what the business would have earned if the storm had not happened. That requires records from before the loss and during the interruption.</p>

<table>
<thead><tr><th>Record</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Monthly profit and loss statements</td><td>Show the normal pattern of revenue and expenses</td></tr>
<tr><td>Prior-year results for the same months</td><td>Establish seasonality, which is critical for tourism and hospitality</td></tr>
<tr><td>Federal tax returns</td><td>Provide an independent check on reported income</td></tr>
<tr><td>Daily or weekly sales reports</td><td>Show exactly when revenue dropped and how it recovered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Payroll records</td><td>Support continuing payroll as a normal operating expense where covered</td></tr>
<tr><td>Contracts, bookings, and cancellations</td><td>Show revenue lost because of the damage</td></tr>
<tr><td>A dated interruption log</td><td>Records closures, partial operations, reopening steps, and the reasons for each</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<p>Many business income forms have a waiting period before coverage begins and pay for a defined period of restoration. They may also require you to resume operations, in whole or in part, as soon as reasonably possible. Keep notes showing the steps you took to reopen and the obstacles you faced, such as permit timelines, contractor availability, or utility outages. Involve your accountant early on larger claims.</p>

