<h2>Industry notes for Corpus Christi businesses</h2>

<h3>Hotels, short-term rentals, and restaurants</h3>
<p>Hospitality revenue along North Beach, Padre Island, Port Aransas, and Rockport depends heavily on the season. A storm in late summer can wipe out bookings in a period that normally carries the year. Save booking reports, cancellation records, and occupancy data for the same months in prior years. Document guest-facing damage that forces rooms or dining areas out of service, even when the rest of the building is open. Note any time a pool, kitchen, or elevator was down, because those can affect revenue beyond the damaged area.</p>

<h3>Marinas, piers, and waterfront businesses</h3>
<p>Waterfront property takes the brunt of surge and wave action. In NOAA's Storm Events Database, the Hurricane Hanna entry for Coastal Nueces County notes that surge "damaged parts of Corpus Christi Marina and numerous piers along Corpus Christi Bay" (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=912741" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event 912741</a>). For marinas and waterfront businesses, document docks, pilings, utilities, fuel systems, and buildings separately, and record tide and water level data for the event. Whether docks and piers are covered at all, and under which policy, varies widely, so read the property schedule closely. During Hurricane Harvey, the NCEI entry for Nueces County describes storm tides of 6 to 8 feet in Port Aransas, with numerous boats damaged or destroyed and pushed out of their moorings onto high ground (<a href="https://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/stormevents/eventdetails.jsp?id=720926" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NCEI event 720926</a>). Boats and vessels are usually insured under separate marine policies, so keep those claims organized on their own track. Our page for <a href="/service-areas/port-aransas-tx/">Port Aransas</a> has more on island and waterfront claims.</p>

<h3>Industrial, warehouse, and port-area properties</h3>
<p>Industrial and warehouse buildings often have large low-slope or metal roofs, tall walls, overhead doors, and specialized equipment. Document roof and wall panel damage across the whole building, not only the obvious areas. For equipment, record whether damage came from wind, water intrusion, or power events, and keep repair technician reports. Production logs and shipping records can support business income claims tied to downtime.</p>

<h3>Retail and office</h3>
<p>For tenants, check your lease to see whether you or the landlord are responsible for insuring and repairing each part of the space. Tenant improvements and betterments may belong on the tenant's policy. Coordinate documentation with the landlord so the same damage is not overlooked by both.</p>

<h2>Check deductibles, limits, and coinsurance early</h2>

<p>Commercial policies often carry several deductibles: a standard deductible, a separate wind and hail or named-storm deductible that may be a percentage of the insured value, and a flood deductible on the NFIP or private flood policy. A percentage deductible on a large building can be substantial, so it is worth knowing the figure before you plan repairs. Our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> shows how a percentage deductible translates into dollars.</p>

<p>Look also at the limits for each coverage and at any coinsurance or agreed value provision. Coinsurance can reduce a payment if the building was insured for less than the percentage of its value the policy requires. These terms differ between policies, so read the declarations and forms together, or have them reviewed, before the claim is presented.</p>

<h2>Tie the damage to the storm</h2>

<p>Insurers will want to know when the damage happened and what caused it. Weather records can help. The NOAA Storm Events Database, National Weather Service storm reports, and NOAA tide data can show what was reported or measured near your property on the date of loss. Our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> searches recent and historical reports by location, and our article on <a href="/noaa-weather-records-document-property-damage-corpus-christi/">using NOAA weather records</a> explains what those records can and cannot prove.</p>

<h2>Common documentation mistakes</h2>
<ul>
<li>Starting cleanup before photographing the damage.</li>
<li>Throwing out damaged inventory without a list or photos.</li>
<li>Waiting months to gather financial records for business income.</li>
<li>Not separating wind damage from flood or surge damage.</li>
<li>Letting a contractor's invoice stand in for a detailed, line-item estimate.</li>
<li>Communicating only by phone and keeping no written record.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How a public adjuster helps with commercial claims</h2>

<p>Commercial claims often involve several policies, large estimates, and financial analysis. Serving Corpus Christi, Nueces County, and surrounding Coastal Bend communities, Corpus Christi Public Adjuster can help organize the building, contents, and business income components, inspect and document the damage, prepare detailed estimates, coordinate with your accountant, and present the claim to the insurer. When the disagreement is over the amount of loss, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may be an option. We cannot promise a particular result, and coverage depends on your policies.</p>

